From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: a733: display: three consistency fixes on paths silicon has not walked

[Recipe cut: regenerated: the hunks that touched the removed debugfs knobs
are gone.]

Items 2, 6 and 7 of a review of the four display modules, found by reading
them against each other rather than on a board. None is a
regression, none blocks anything, and none of the three sits on the path
a test boot proved -- that boot trains the link from cold, follows the cable and
puts a login prompt on /dev/fb0 with zero err or warn lines from any of the
four modules. What the three have in common is that each is a promise the code
makes in one file and does not keep in another.

⚠ LINE NUMBERS. Every sun60i-*.c number below is the file as it stands with the
full patches/ stack (`clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`…`arm64-dts-a733-sbu-edge-from-the-dp-transmitter-to-the-typec-receptacle`) applied to pristine 6.18.36 -- the tree this
patch was written and compiled against. `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths` did not exist yet at that moment and
takes sun60i-dp.c, so its numbers there will have moved; every site is named by
function as well, for exactly that reason. Kernel-core numbers (drm_edid.c,
drm_probe_helper.c, drm_atomic_helper.c) are 6.18.36 as shipped.

FIX 1 -- THE TRANSMITTER LATCHES A DEPTH THE TCON WILL NOT PROGRAM
-------------------------------------------------------------------

WHAT IS WRONG. sun60i-dp.c:3193-3194, inside sun60i_dp_bridge_atomic_enable()
(:3150):

	if (connector->display_info.bpc)
		dp->bpc = connector->display_info.bpc;

That guard makes `bpc == 0` mean "keep whatever was there". The other half of
the same hardware invariant, sun60i_kms_crtc_bpc() (sun60i-kms.c:504-515),
makes it mean 8:

	if (!connector || !connector->display_info.bpc)
		return 8;

And nothing ever resets dp->bpc. It is 8 at probe (sun60i-dp.c:3512) and from
then on only ever assigned; sun60i_dp_aux_disable() does not clear it, so the
value survives a Type-C disconnect, an AUX teardown and a physical unplug.

THE SCENARIO -- two sinks, in this order:

  1. Modeset on a sink whose EDID is 1.4 and carries a colour-depth field, say
     10 bpc. Both sides read display_info.bpc, MISC0 (sun60i-dp.c:769) and
     TV_PIXELDEPTH_MODE (sun60i-tcon-tv.c:449) agree on 10.
  2. Unplug. dp->bpc stays 10.
  3. Plug a sink whose EDID is 1.3. update_display_info() (drm_edid.c:6671)
     parses the depth field only for revision >= 4 -- "Only defined for 1.4
     with digital displays", the gate at drm_edid.c:6718-6720 -- and the EDID
     1.3 fallback above it only fires for a DFP-1.x TMDS sink. So a plain 1.3
     sink leaves display_info.bpc at 0. That is a NORMAL DISPLAY, not a failure.
     TCON_TV1 is then programmed for 8 (sun60i-kms.c:568 -> sun60i-tcon-tv.c:1049
     -> :449). The transmitter keeps 10.

The two blocks now describe different pixel depths for one stream. That is the
disagreement this driver already names: SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS != 0, which
the comment ten lines above the defect itself (sun60i-dp.c:3184) calls a
mode-programming error.

⚠ AND THE COMMENT IN sun60i-kms.c WAS WRONG, which is the half that would have
stopped the next reader from finding this. It claimed "both sides take the
number from one place". They read the same FIELD; they do not apply the same
rule to it, and the DP side holds a private latched copy the KMS side cannot
see. Left standing, that comment turns a defect into documented behaviour.

WHAT THIS CHANGES. One line, and the fallback rule becomes the same on both
sides:

	dp->bpc = connector->display_info.bpc ?: 8;

This is the expression sun60i_dp_bridge_mode_valid() already applies to the same
field one screen earlier (sun60i-dp.c:2981, `info->bpc ? info->bpc : 8`), so the
file now answers `bpc == 0` one way instead of two. The sun60i-kms.c comment is
corrected in place -- the false claim removed, the shared fallback named -- not
left to become accidentally true.

⚠ FALSIFIER, AND IT IS NOT CHEAP. This cannot be proved with one sink. It needs
a SINK CHANGE: an EDID-1.4 display carrying a colour-depth field, then an
EDID-1.3 display without one, on a connector that reaches .atomic_enable twice.
Every sink measured on this tree so far has answered bpc != 0, which is exactly
why the latch has never shown. `edid-decode < /sys/class/drm/card0-DP-1/edid`
on BOTH sinks first, to confirm one of them really carries no depth field --
without that step a green run proves nothing.

FIX 2 -- commit->arm()'s RESULT REACHES A CALLER AGAIN
--------------------------------------------------------

WHAT IS WRONG. struct sun60i_de_commit documents its third op as "@arm: make the
batch take effect, and report whether it did" (sun60i-de.c:366; the op at :387).
Three of five callers honour that: sun60i_de_start() (:1295) turns a failure
into an error and a goto, and the `background` (:1503) and `ccsc` (:1556) verbs
hand it back to userspace. The two on the KMS path do not --
sun60i_de_layer_disable() (:1107) and sun60i_de_layer_enable() (:1134) were void
and dropped it, so sun60i_de_kms_scanout() (:1779), which IS the page flip,
returned 0 unconditionally.

⚠ THIS IS LATENT AND THE PATCH CLAIMS NO BENEFIT IT CANNOT SHOW.
sun60i_de_mmio_arm() (:526) is a read-to-order and a `return 0` -- it cannot
fail, so today nothing is lost and no log line changes. What makes it worth a
patch now is what the indirection exists for, stated in its own kerneldoc: an
RCQ backend behind the same three ops. Behind such a backend every page flip
would report success whether or not the commit landed, and the one line that
would say otherwise -- the drm_err in sun60i_kms_plane_atomic_update()
(sun60i-kms.c:414) -- could never fire. The defect would then arrive together
with the backend, inside a patch about something else, already invisible.

WHAT THIS CHANGES. Both helpers become int and return what arm() reported.
sun60i_de_kms_scanout() returns that instead of 0, which makes sun60i-kms.c:414
reachable. The debugfs `layer` verb (:1593-1595) returns it too -- and that is
what makes any of this testable from a board at all, because `echo 1 > layer` is
this driver's own probe that a CPU store reaches a cold DE's datapath, and until
now it could not report a failed commit either.

Two callers still drop the value, deliberately, each commented at the call:

  sun60i_de_stop() (:1339)          quiesce and reset have to happen whether or
                                    not the layer-off committed; a stop that
                                    refuses to stop leaves the block fetching
                                    from pages about to be handed back
  sun60i_de_kms_layer_off() (:1801) its caller is .atomic_disable, which returns
                                    void to the atomic helpers and has nowhere
                                    to put an error either

⚠ de->layer_on IS STILL ASSIGNED UNCONDITIONALLY. What that flag should say
after a commit that did not land is a real question, and it is one no backend in
this tree can yet pose. Answering it here would change the behaviour of a path
silicon has walked, on the strength of a failure mode that cannot occur yet.

⚠ FALSIFIER: there is none a board can run today, and saying so is the honest
answer. With mmio_arm() unable to fail, every path through this change returns
what it returned before -- that is precisely why it is safe, and precisely why
it proves nothing. It becomes testable the day a backend that can fail exists,
and the test is then a forced failure in arm() producing a `scanout from ...`
drm_err where before there was silence.

FIX 3 -- A MODE FILTER MUST NOT LOG AT ERROR
----------------------------------------------

WHAT IS WRONG. sun60i_tcon_tv_check_mode() (sun60i-tcon-tv.c:292) refuses ten
mode conditions silently, by bare `return -EINVAL`, and one -- interlace,
:326-329 -- with a dev_err. It is reached from sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_check()
(:1028), i.e. from the CRTC's .mode_valid, which the core calls once per mode
per connector probe and again on every atomic check.

sun60i-dp.c states the doctrine this contradicts at its own .detect: "no sink is
a STATE, not an error", and that path "logs nothing on the ten-second poll"
(sun60i-dp.c:3055-3056). sun60i_dp_bridge_mode_valid() follows it and logs
nothing at all.

⚠ THE REVIEW'S TRIGGER FOR THIS ITEM DOES NOT EXIST, AND ITEM 7 IS REFUTED HERE.
It says a sink offering 1080i/576i produces an error line per interlaced mode on
every probe, every ten seconds under poll. It does not, and the reason is in the
same driver. Both mode walks ask the BRIDGE CHAIN BEFORE THE CRTC and skip the
CRTC as soon as a bridge refuses -- drm_probe_helper.c:118-127 (`continue`) and
drm_atomic_helper.c:529-537 (early return) -- and sun60i_dp_bridge_mode_valid()
answers MODE_NO_INTERLACE (sun60i-dp.c:2995) first, every time. The only other
caller, sun60i_tcon_tv_enable() (:647), reads tcon->mode, which is either the
progressive built-in constant (:1206) or an adjusted_mode that has already been
through that same filter; there is no debugfs setter for tcon->mode. The branch
is unreachable in this tree. No flood is possible, and none was observed.

WHY IT IS STILL WORTH THE LINE. The check stays; only the level drops, to
DRM_UT_KMS via drm_dev_dbg(tcon->dev, ...) -- the exact expansion of
drm_dbg_kms(), used with the struct device this module has instead of a
drm_device it does not (precedent: drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/panel-mipi-dbi.c:144).
This is the generic, last-line-of-defence check on a CRTC, and it goes live the
moment a second output without an interlace filter of its own hangs off that
CRTC -- which is the case a CRTC-level check exists for. Fixing the level while
the branch is dead costs one line; fixing it after it is live costs somebody
else a log flood during their bring-up.

⚠ NO DIAGNOSTIC IS DELETED; the line stays. The message stays in the
source and comes back with `drm.debug=0x04` or
`dyndbg="func sun60i_tcon_tv_check_mode +p"`, no rebuild -- and
sun60i_tcon_tv_enable() still prints its own dev_err naming the mode (:649-652)
on any refusal, so the one path a user can drive keeps an ERROR line.

⚠ FALSIFIER. Showing the change DID something needs both a sink that offers
interlaced modes at all and a second, non-DP output on the same CRTC. Neither
exists on this board. Showing it broke nothing is the cheap half and is what the
build covers: the four objects build and no other caller of check_mode changed.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE, AND THAT IS THE POINT
---------------------------------------------

The path the test boot proved -- cold boot, alt mode, AUX, EDID, link training,
TCON, DE, /dev/fb0, fbcon -- is the same path after this patch, write for write:

  - Fix 1 alters dp->bpc ONLY when display_info.bpc == 0. On every sink measured
    on this tree it is non-zero, and old and new code then assign the identical
    value. The board's own sink is one of those.
  - Fix 2 changes no register write, no ordering and no value. It changes two
    return types and turns `return 0` into `return ret` where ret is provably 0
    for the only backend that exists (sun60i_de_mmio_arm(), :526).
  - Fix 3 changes a log level on a branch nothing reaches.

So this patch is expected to be a NO-OP on silicon. A test boot that shows any
difference in the boot sequence is evidence AGAINST it, not for it. The
acceptance criterion is sameness: same trained link, same mode list, same
/dev/fb0, same zero err/warn lines from the four modules.

BUILD
------

Pristine 6.18.36 + the full patches/ stack + this one, configured from
configs/a733_defconfig, `make ARCH=arm64 drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/` on the native
aarch64 host: sun60i-dp.o, sun60i-de.o, sun60i-kms.o and sun60i-tcon-tv.o all
compile, zero warnings. Only a measurement proves an effect, so this shows that the patch applies
and compiles and nothing more; all three fixes are unproven on silicon.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE
----------------------------------

Items 1, 3, 4, 5 and 8 of the same review. They are five different kinds of
change -- a use-after-free, a missing lock, two unwind paths and a register
ownership question -- and each is worth its own bisect point. Items 1 and 8 in
particular land in sun60i-dp.c's .attach and in sun60i_dp_video_configure(),
which `drm-a733-dp-repair-three-error-paths` is taking; this patch therefore stays out of both functions and
touches sun60i-dp.c in exactly one place, inside .atomic_enable.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
@@ -1082,14 +1082,17 @@ static void sun60i_de_layer(struct sun60i_de *de)
 			sun60i_de_attctl());
 }
 
-static void sun60i_de_layer_disable(struct sun60i_de *de)
+static int sun60i_de_layer_disable(struct sun60i_de *de)
 {
 	u32 ovl = sun60i_de_chn(DE_VI0_SLOT) + DE_CHN_OVL;
+	int ret;
 
 	de->commit->begin(de);
 	sun60i_de_stage(de, ovl + OVL_LAY_ATTCTL(DE_OVL_LAYER), 0);
-	de->commit->arm(de);
+	ret = de->commit->arm(de);
 	de->layer_on = false;
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -1108,13 +1111,24 @@ static void sun60i_de_layer_disable(struct sun60i_de *de)
  * ⚠ THE ORDER IS NOT OPTIONAL, and it is the correction to a measurement that
  * had to be withdrawn and redone: prove the observer, THEN move a register. A
  * screen nobody has confirmed is on cannot falsify anything.
+ *
+ * Both helpers return what @arm reported, because that is the only place in
+ * either of them that can fail; every caller that has somewhere to put an
+ * error now does. de->layer_on is still assigned unconditionally: what the
+ * flag should say after a commit that did not land is a question no backend
+ * in this tree can yet pose, and inventing an answer here would change a path
+ * silicon has walked.
  */
-static void sun60i_de_layer_enable(struct sun60i_de *de)
+static int sun60i_de_layer_enable(struct sun60i_de *de)
 {
+	int ret;
+
 	de->commit->begin(de);
 	sun60i_de_layer(de);
-	de->commit->arm(de);
+	ret = de->commit->arm(de);
 	de->layer_on = true;
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -1218,6 +1232,13 @@ static int sun60i_de_start(struct sun60i_de *de)
  */
 static void sun60i_de_stop(struct sun60i_de *de)
 {
+	/*
+	 * One of the two places the commit result is deliberately dropped (the
+	 * other is sun60i_de_kms_layer_off()): quiesce and reset are what has
+	 * to happen next whether or not the layer-off landed, and a stop that
+	 * refuses to stop leaves the block fetching from pages that are about
+	 * to belong to somebody else.
+	 */
 	sun60i_de_layer_disable(de);
 	sun60i_de_quiesce(de);
 	sun60i_de_clocks_off(de);
@@ -1412,9 +1433,8 @@ int sun60i_de_kms_scanout(struct sun60i_de *de, dma_addr_t scanout, u32 pitch)
 
 	de->fb_dma = scanout;
 	de->fb_pitch = pitch;
-	sun60i_de_layer_enable(de);
 
-	return 0;
+	return sun60i_de_layer_enable(de);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(sun60i_de_kms_scanout);
 
@@ -1425,6 +1445,11 @@ void sun60i_de_kms_layer_off(struct sun60i_de *de)
 	if (!de->enabled)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * Dropped for the same reason as in sun60i_de_stop(), and for one more:
+	 * the caller is .atomic_disable, which returns void to the atomic
+	 * helpers and has nowhere to put an error either.
+	 */
 	sun60i_de_layer_disable(de);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(sun60i_de_kms_layer_off);
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -2666,8 +2666,20 @@ static void sun60i_dp_bridge_atomic_enable(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	 * mismatch is the one the driver already reads and prints.
 	 */
 	drm_mode_copy(&dp->mode, &crtc_state->adjusted_mode);
-	if (connector->display_info.bpc)
-		dp->bpc = connector->display_info.bpc;
+
+	/*
+	 * ⚠ THE FALLBACK IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE INVARIANT ABOVE, NOT A
+	 * DEFAULT VALUE. TCON_TV1 takes its depth from sun60i_kms_crtc_bpc(),
+	 * which answers 8 for a sink that reports none, so a transmitter that
+	 * instead KEPT its previous value would describe a different pixel
+	 * depth than the raster feeding it -- the SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS
+	 * disagreement named above. bpc == 0 is a real sink and not an error:
+	 * EDID 1.3 has no bit-depth field at all, so it is what
+	 * drm_add_display_info() leaves behind for every pre-1.4 display.
+	 * sun60i_dp_bridge_mode_valid() already applies exactly this fallback
+	 * to the same field; this was the one place that did not.
+	 */
+	dp->bpc = connector->display_info.bpc ?: 8;
 
 	/* Auto on both: the rate ladder asks the sink and the PHY, and the lane
 	 * ladder is the fallback DP 1.4 section 3.5.1.2.1 requires. A modeset must not
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
@@ -500,6 +500,12 @@ static int sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_check(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
  * sun60i_dp_bridge_atomic_enable() reads exactly this field for its own half,
  * so both sides take the number from one place -- which is what retires the
  * constant in sun60i-display.h that has held the invariant until now.
+ *
+ * ⚠ THE FALLBACK IS PART OF WHAT IS SHARED. "One place" holds only while
+ * both sides answer bpc == 0 with the SAME number, and until `drm-a733-three-consistency-fixes` they did
+ * not: the transmitter kept whatever the previous sink had reported. The two
+ * 8s -- this one and the one in sun60i_dp_bridge_atomic_enable() -- move
+ * together or not at all.
  */
 static u8 sun60i_kms_crtc_bpc(struct sun60i_kms *kms,
 			      struct drm_atomic_state *state)
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-tcon-tv.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-tcon-tv.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-tcon-tv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-tcon-tv.c
@@ -58,6 +58,7 @@
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 
 #include <drm/drm_modes.h>
+#include <drm/drm_print.h>
 
 #include "sun60i-display.h"
 #include "sun60i-kms.h"
@@ -306,7 +307,35 @@ static int sun60i_tcon_tv_check_mode(struct sun60i_tcon_tv *tcon,
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	if (mode->flags & DRM_MODE_FLAG_INTERLACE) {
-		dev_err(tcon->dev, "interlaced modes are not implemented here\n");
+		/*
+		 * ⚠ DEBUG, NOT ERROR, BECAUSE THIS FUNCTION IS A MODE FILTER.
+		 * sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_check() runs it from the CRTC's .mode_valid,
+		 * which the core calls once per mode per connector probe -- every
+		 * ten seconds under poll -- and again on every atomic check. A
+		 * refusal there is the filter doing its job, not a fault, and it
+		 * has to stay cheap for every mode a sink offers.
+		 *
+		 * ⚠ NO PATH REACHES THIS BRANCH TODAY, so this is a level fix on a
+		 * defensive check and not a fix for an observed log flood. Both
+		 * mode walks ask the bridge chain BEFORE the CRTC and skip the CRTC
+		 * entirely once a bridge has refused (drm_probe_helper.c:118-127,
+		 * drm_atomic_helper.c:529-537), and sun60i_dp_bridge_mode_valid()
+		 * answers MODE_NO_INTERLACE first. The other caller,
+		 * sun60i_tcon_tv_enable(), only ever sees the progressive built-in
+		 * mode or an adjusted_mode that has already been through that same
+		 * filter. What makes this branch live is a second output on this
+		 * CRTC with no interlace filter of its own -- which is exactly what
+		 * a generic CRTC-level check exists for, and why it has to be right
+		 * before then rather than after.
+		 *
+		 * Demoted, not deleted: drm.debug=0x04, or a dyndbg on this
+		 * function, brings the line back with no rebuild -- and
+		 * sun60i_tcon_tv_enable() prints its own dev_err naming the mode on
+		 * any refusal, so the user-facing path keeps an ERROR line
+		 * either way.
+		 */
+		drm_dev_dbg(tcon->dev, DRM_UT_KMS,
+			    "interlaced modes are not implemented here\n");
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 
